<!--
  network.xml — the WAN-permitted libvirt network the GHOSTS guest lives on
  (#325). Defined by `install-network.sh net-setup`.

  ┌─ WHY THERE IS A <forward> ELEMENT, UNLIKE EVERY OTHER SANDBOX NETWORK ─┐
  │ Every other detonation route in this repo (sandbox/windows's           │
  │ sandbox-network.xml, sandbox/network.xml for the Linux runner) has no  │
  │ <forward> — its absence is what makes them isolated. This network      │
  │ inverts that on purpose: GHOSTS' realism (actual browsing, actual      │
  │ document downloads) requires actual WAN egress, decided in #300 and    │
  │ #331. Do not "fix" this network by removing the <forward> — that would │
  │ break GHOSTS entirely, and does not belong here regardless: the        │
  │ containment for this network is the iptables policy in                 │
  │ network-filter.sh, not the absence of a route out.                     │
  │                                                                        │
  │ Scoped to the WAN-facing interface only (dev='ens9f0', the host's      │
  │ lower-metric default route) rather than left to whichever interface    │
  │ the kernel happens to route through, so the NAT/forwarding rules       │
  │ libvirt generates are unambiguous about which physical link this       │
  │ traffic leaves on.                                                     │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  This element alone does not implement "WAN yes, LAN no" — a NAT'd libvirt
  network forwards to anywhere the host can route to, LAN included.
  network-filter.sh is what actually blocks RFC1918 destinations, with one
  exception: this bridge's own gateway (10.20.30.1), which is also where
  the GHOSTS API publishes its port (#324) — see network-filter.sh's header
  for why it lives there and not on a docker-internal address. Re-apply it
  after every `net-setup`, and verify with verify-network-isolation.sh
  before this network ever carries a real (non-benign-test) sample:

    virsh net-dumpxml ghosts | grep -c forward         # must print 1 (here)
    iptables -S FORWARD | grep virbr-ghosts             # the DROP/ACCEPT pairs
    sandbox/ghosts/verify-network-isolation.sh          # from inside a guest

  DNS is real, deliberately: this guest needs to resolve actual hostnames,
  unlike every other sandbox guest which gets INetSim/FakeNet's fake
  answers. No dhcp-option=6 override here — the guest gets this bridge's
  own address (10.20.30.1) as its resolver, which is libvirt's dnsmasq,
  which forwards upstream using the host's own resolver. The RFC1918-block
  rule in network-filter.sh is an IP-range policy, not a hostname policy, so
  it holds regardless of what a lookup resolves to (including
  DNS-rebinding attempts pointing a real hostname at a private address).
-->
<network xmlns:dnsmasq='http://libvirt.org/schemas/network/dnsmasq/1.0'>
  <name>ghosts</name>
  <bridge name='virbr-ghosts' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <forward mode='nat' dev='ens9f0'>
    <nat>
      <!-- Real HTTP/HTTPS/whatever-the-timeline-needs, not just port 80/443:
           GHOSTS' browser/document handlers are not port-restricted, and
           the RFC1918 block in network-filter.sh is what actually contains
           this, not a port allowlist here. -->
      <port start='1' end='65535'/>
    </nat>
  </forward>

  <!-- 10.20.30.0/24: distinct from every other range already in use on
       this host (10.8.0.0/24 wg0, 10.10.10.0/24 sandbox, 10.90.0.0/24
       ghosts_net docker, 192.168.42.0/24 LAN, 198.18.0.0/24 virbr-hpsbx),
       so a routing mistake elsewhere can't silently overlap this one. -->
  <ip address='10.20.30.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <!-- #327 (the VM domain issue) has now chosen the GHOSTS guest's MAC
           (win11-ghosts-kvm.xml) - pinned here the same way
           sandbox-network.xml pins win11-sandbox's: a second guest getting
           a lease on this bridge should fail, not share the network, the
           same reasoning that applies to every other single-guest sandbox
           network in this repo. The range stays too, wide enough to still
           leave room for a throwaway test guest (verify-network-isolation.sh
           boots one) without colliding with the pinned address. -->
      <range start='10.20.30.10' end='10.20.30.99'/>
      <host mac='00:1a:a0:3c:4d:6f' name='win11-ghosts' ip='10.20.30.50'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
  <domain name='ghosts.local'/>
</network>
